Design and Analysis a) Order of constructors and destructors
Stack objects are destroyed automatically when they leave their scope.
Block-scope objects are destroyed when the block ends.
Heap objects are destroyed only when delete is used.
Destructors execute in reverse order of object construction within the same scope. b) What happens if delete is forgotten?
If dynamically allocated memory is not released, a memory leak occurs. The program continues without freeing that memory.

c) What happens if delete is used instead of delete[]?

When an array is allocated using new[], it must be released using delete[]. Using delete instead causes undefined behaviour.

d) Why does a destructor take no arguments and cannot be overloaded?

A destructor has a fixed name, ~ClassName(), and takes no parameters. A class can have only one destructor because it performs a single object-cleanup operation. 7. Memory Layout of the Seat Map For a 2 × 3 auditorium: Auditorium Object +------------------+ | rows = 2 | | seats = 3 | | seatMap pointer | | booked = 2 | +--------|---------+ | v Row Pointer Array +-------+-------+ | Row 0 | Row 1 | +---|---+---|---+ | | v v +-------+ +-------+ | O X O | | O O X | +-------+ +-------+

